  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1238 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nTonight and Sunday Morning\n\nCurrently watching a shortwave trough over eastern Montana. The \n08.12z models are in general agreement that this wave will move \nsoutheast through the area overnight tonight and on Sunday morning. \nWhile there is general agreement with the upper level features,\nthere is plenty of uncertainty in the CAMs on how convection \nwill initiate and where it will track from tonight into Monday\nmorning.\n\nSome CAMs suggest that storms will develop near the North \nDakota/South Dakota/Minnesota borders during the mid- to late \nevening. Over time as cold pools coalesce develop this \nconvection will form into a line will track southeast along and \nsouth of Interstate 94 overnight and on Sunday morning. In this\nscenario, strong to severe straight-line winds would be \nfavorable. However, the timing of this line does not look favorable\nfor widespread severe winds due to the instability gradually \nwaning overnight. \n\nOther CAMs suggest that the MCS, currently over Nebraska, will move \neast across Iowa. Eventually a linear MCS moves east across Iowa \ntonight. Like the previous scenario, the primary severe weather \nthreat would be damaging winds. However, this time it would be \nmainly south of Interstate 80. \n\nLow temperatures tonight will range from the mid-50s to around 60 \nalong and north of Interstate 94, and in the mid-60s elsewhere.\n\nSunday afternoon and night\n\nAnother shortwave trough, just moving into Washington State, will \nmove east southeast through areas areas along and south of \nInterstate afternoon during the afternoon and evening. The models \nsuggest that there will be some recovery during the afternoon. How \nmuch recovery is still highly uncertain. Surface-based CAPES will \nlikely range 1-3K J/kg in parts of southeast Minnesota, \nnortheast Iowa, and southwest Wisconsin. The better instability \nwill be found along and south of a weak boundary in this area. \nWith 0-6 km shear forecast to be in the 35 to 45 knot range, \nscattered supercells will be possible. With the better speed \nshear being located in the 0-3 km layer, damaging winds will be \nthe primary threat with maybe an isolated tornado threat.\n\nWith warm-layer clouds depths around 4 km and precipitable water \nvalues from 1.5 to 2.5 inches, any showers and storms will be highly \nefficient rain producers. Fortunately, the storm motions should be \nfast enough to minimize the flooding threat.\n\nHigh temperatures on Sunday will range from the mid-70s to lower \n80s. Low temperatures on Sunday night will range from mid-50s to \naround 60 along and north of Interstate 94 and in the mid-60s \nelsewhere.\n\nMonday afternoon and Monday night\n\nSome weak cyclogenesis is forecast to develop over eastern South \nDakota. A front will extend east southeast across southern \nMinnesota, northern Iowa, northern Illinois, and southern Wisconsin. \nSurface-based CAPES will be 2-3K J/kg south of this front and up to \n2K J/kg north of this front. With unidirectional westerly flow \nin the mid-levels, there is a potential for pockets of damaging \nwinds in any bowing cells.\n\nWith warm-layer clouds depths around 4 km and precipitable water \nvalues between 1.5 and 2 inches, any showers and storms will be \nhighly efficient rain producers. With the front not expected to move \nmuch, there could be some training cells which would increase the \nflooding threat.\n\nHigh temperatures on Monday will range from the around 80 to mid-\n80s. Low temperatures on Monday night will range from mid-50s to \naround 60 along and north of Interstate 94 and in the mid-60s \nelsewhere.\n\nTuesday into Wednesday\n\nA front will continue to meander south of Interstate 94. As \nshortwaves track east southeast across the area, there will be \nperiodic showers and storms. Strong zonal flow in the mid-levels may \nresult in some periods of damaging winds. In addition, with \nprecipitable water values in the 1.5 to 2 inch range, these \nshowers and storms will be highly efficient rain producers.\n\nHigh temperatures on Tuesday will range from the mid-70s to mid-80s. \nLow temperatures on Tuesday night will range from mid-50s to around \n60 along and north of Interstate 94 and in the mid-60s elsewhere.",
